A||N||3||kinboyatuwo||NOS in A Duke?||||5||
Z||000000||kinboyatuwo||10-05-2000||12:38 PM||||Has anyone done NOS in a Duke?<P>If so what kind of hp gain?<BR>What kits?<BR>Is it expensive?<BR>And is it a good idea or should i get a chopped off Turbo off another car?<P><BR>Curious and maybee thinking of doing one or the other (after the last snow fall)<BR>thanks <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||10-05-2000||02:02 PM||||My thoughts:<P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.nosnitrous.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nosnitrous.com</A>  for good information.  I didn't see a NOS kit specifically for the 2.5, but they offer kits for the throttle-body injected 2.8, 4.3 Vortec, and 5.0/5.7 Chevy, and they claim to offer applications for "almost any" car, so I would think it wouldn't be too much trouble to get a kit for the Duke.<P>The GM TBI kits look like they run from $500-$750 - not cheap, but a lot cheaper than an engine swap.<P>I think running more than a 50hp shot on a stock Duke is asking for trouble in the form of a thrown rod or blown head gasket.  And if you've got a high-mileage Duke or one that doesn't generate good compression on all 4 cylinders, I'd forget it.  I briefly considered NOS, but my Duke has 115K on it and I don't want to risk a meltdown.<P>Good luck!  If you decide to nitrousize your Duke, let us know how it goes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||10-05-2000||04:46 PM||||Xtreme fiero has nitrous n his 4cyl...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Xtreme Fiero's||10-05-2000||06:09 PM||||<BR>1FST2M6: HAD!!!!<P>kinboyatuwo: I used a direct port AKA the Sportsman Fogger (#741-05088) modified to use only 4 of the nozzles (13700B). the system I used was WAY to much for any stock motor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> if you wanted to put NOS on your duke I would get the Nitrous Works Universal EFI system ($439 from jeg's). Just remember to turn up your fuel pressure regulator (stock regulator YES) a little bit (like one Turn) You will need to make sure your engine is it top shape b-4 you try it though. ||||reg||1
